    "knowledge_point": "The Domain of a Cylinder",
    "problem": "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$",
    "worked_solution": "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(0,-1).$",
